{"answer":"No. At the permitted input n=40, n^2+n+41=1681=41·41, with 1<41<1681. Thus the value is composite, so this single counterexample refutes the statement that the polynomial is prime for every nonnegative integer n. No external facts or sources are required.","approaches":[{"description":"Use a permitted input that yields an explicit nontrivial factorization, then apply the counterexample rule for a universal statement.","id":"factor-counterexample","outcome":"candidate","reason":"n=40 gives the checkable factorization 1681=41·41."},{"description":"Check only an early value such as n=0.","id":"single-positive-instance","outcome":"incomplete","reason":"Even if one selected value is prime, that establishes only that instance and cannot prove a claim quantified over every nonnegative integer."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question asks: \"Is n^2+n+41 prime for every nonnegative integer n?\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"stated-domain","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The asserted domain is all nonnegative integers, and the expression under consideration is n^2+n+41.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by direct substitution and arithmetic in step-evaluate-forty.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"value-at-forty","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"At n=40, the polynomial has value 1681.","step_ids":["step-evaluate-forty"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the nontrivial factorization in step-factor-value.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["value-at-forty"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"composite-counterexample","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The value at n=40 is composite.","step_ids":["step-factor-value"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the counterexample rule applied in step-refute-universal.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["stated-domain","composite-counterexample"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"universal-statement-false","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The statement that n^2+n+41 is prime for every nonnegative integer n is false.","step_ids":["step-refute-universal"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-select-forty","justification":"40 is a nonnegative integer because 40≥0, so it is in the stated domain.","statement":"Choose n=40 as a permitted input."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-select-forty"],"id":"step-evaluate-forty","justification":"Direct arithmetic gives 40^2+40+41=1600+40+41=1681.","statement":"Evaluate the polynomial at n=40."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-evaluate-forty"],"id":"step-factor-value","justification":"41·41=1681, and 1<41<1681. Hence 1681 has a divisor other than 1 and itself and is composite.","statement":"Factor the resulting value as 1681=41·41."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-select-forty","step-factor-value"],"id":"step-refute-universal","justification":"A universal assertion over a domain requires its predicate to hold at every member of that domain. Here 40 is in the domain, but the corresponding value is composite rather than prime; therefore the universal assertion is false.","statement":"Apply the counterexample rule to the universal statement."}],"question_status":"refuted","tool_requests":[]}
